{"author":{"id":"a3a282966ced55effb4560271fcd98e13241af24ea76b00ec76a0f18154bc8ee"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1790976773,"nonce":"+Y21AkOiLC/SbK0HoUlIZJgRv3E=","message":"Decided and landed as 3797a951. The reasoning is `doc/design/configurable-schema.md` D8, next to D2's built-ins; `config-entity.md` E4 and AGENTS.md say four built-ins now.\n\nWhat is in code:\n\n- `schema.Builtins` carries `rank` (kind `rank`, ordinal -5, so it sorts with the other built-ins and before every configured field). Every type gets it in `Compile`, with no field entity, so `allocation` and `iteration` have it too and the write check accepts it everywhere.\n- Null until a drag writes one. `lessByRank` is the one rule: a set rank first, ties by id; a null rank last. **Two nulls are equal**, not ordered by id — decided beyond the brief, because ordering unranked issues by hash would have thrown away the query's own order (last edited first) on every view of a store nobody has dragged anything in, which is every view today. The id tie-break stays where it earns its keep: two equal set ranks, which is the concurrent-drag case of 441dcbb.\n- `view/kinds.go`: `rank` is a defaulted argument, `\"rank\"`, on list, board and gantt (matrix has none). `FieldKey` refuses an empty string and `null` means \"use the default\", so a view cannot ask for no rank at all: the three \"no rank: cannot reorder\" paths were unreachable and are **removed**, with the `rankKey != \"\"` branches around them. The argument stays for a second ordering field.\n- The call line hides `rank=rank` while it is the default, the way it hides `fields`: the order of the rows already says it. A named rank shows like any other argument.\n- The `keys.grab` label is \"on id, card, bar: grab / drop (move it)\"; the hints always offer `space: grab` and `↑↓: reorder`.\n\nThe schema file and the store:\n\n- `schema.yaml` and both presets drop their `rank` field and the `rank` anchor. `git work schema init jira|linear` no longer creates one.\n- A file that still lists `rank` as a field of a type is **an override of the built-in**, exactly as a file listing `title` is — the document validator already refuses to change a built-in's kind or give it values, and `compileField` forces the kind — so an old `schema.yaml` imports without creating a duplicate and without an error. `schema export` leaves a built-in out unless an entity overrides its name or description.\n- The eight `*/rank` entities on this tracker (initiative, epic, story, task, decision, bug, subtask, iteration) are **archived**, one `schema archive \u003ctype\u003e/rank` each rather than `import --prune`, which would have been a wider blade than the job needed. Each held exactly the built-in's own name and description, so nothing was lost; `schema export` now prints no rank and `schema import schema.yaml` is a no-op. The archives are local until someone pushes.\n- The jira derive goldens moved with `-update`: the `rank` entries and their \"local-only field: never exported\" notes are gone and the fake entity ids shift by one per type. The sync is unaffected — Jira's own Rank field was never mapped (it is a `gh-lexo-rank` custom field, still a \"not mapped\" warning), and a built-in is skipped by `jira/compile.go` exactly as an unaliased field was, so `rank` stays local-only, never exported and never cleared.\n- `doc/design/allocations.md` A2: an allocation carries the rank every type is born with and ignores it.\n\nOne consequence, recorded in D8: on a store where nothing has a rank, the first drop puts its issue at the top of its group. The answer, if it bites, is the order-preserving renumber `rank` describes.","files":null}]}